    peopleAndChurch: "• Jesus of Nazareth: The Promised Messiah, Redeemer, and King who taught in the Temple courts, suffered in Gethsemane, and rose triumphant outside the walls.\n\n• Simon Peter & The Twelve: Formed the foundational apostolic leadership; boldly testified before the Sanhedrin and guided the rapidly multiplying mother church.\n\n• James the Just: Brother of the Lord, revered for his ascetic piety; served as resident apostle and bishop of Jerusalem, presiding over the Jerusalem Council (Acts 15).\n\n• Stephen & The Seven Deacons: Chosen to administer daily relief to Hellenistic widows; Stephen became the first Christian martyr, dying with words of forgiveness.\n\n• Joseph of Arimathea & Nicodemus: Secret disciples of the Sanhedrin who boldly claimed Christ's body and laid Him in a new rock-hewn garden tomb.\n\n• Mary the Mother of Jesus & The Women: Persevered in constant prayer with the disciples in the Upper Room following the Ascension.\n\n• Annas & Caiaphas: The entrenched Sadducean high priestly dynasty that interrogated Jesus and orchestrated His delivery to Pontius Pilate.",
    politicalInsights: "• Roman Provincial Rule & Prefects (-6 BC to 70 AD): After Herod Archelaus was deposed in 6 AD, Judea was placed under direct equestrian prefects based at Caesarea Maritima. Prefects (such as Pontius Pilate, 26–36 AD) brought military cohorts to Jerusalem during major festivals, garrisoning the Antonia Fortress overlooking the Temple courts.\n\n• Sadducean Aristocracy & The Sanhedrin: The 71-member Great Sanhedrin exercised broad internal civil and religious jurisdiction over Jewish life, though Rome reserved the jus gladii (capital punishment) for the Roman governor.\n\n• Herod Agrippa I & Temporary Client Monarchy (41–44 AD): The grandson of Herod the Great briefly reunited Judea under royal client rule, seeking favor with Jerusalem's orthodox elite by executing James the son of Zebedee and imprisoning Peter.\n\n• Archaeological Verifications: The 1990 discovery of the Caiaphas family ossuary south of Jerusalem, the Temple Warning Inscriptions prohibiting Gentiles from the inner courts on pain of death, and the Pool of Siloam excavation confirm the historical authenticity of New Testament Jerusalem.",
    eraChronology: "• ~4 BC: Death of Herod the Great; division of kingdom among his sons.\n• 6 AD: Judea annexed as Roman province; Quirinius census sparks Zealot unrest.\n• ~8 AD: Twelve-year-old Jesus converses with the doctors in the Temple.\n• 26–36 AD: Pontius Pilate serves as Roman Prefect of Judea.\n• Spring 30 AD: Passion Week, Gethsemane, Crucifixion, Resurrection, and Pentecost.\n• ~34 AD: Martyrdom of Stephen and initial scattering of Hellenistic believers.\n• 41–44 AD: Reign of Herod Agrippa I; martyrdom of James the brother of John.\n• 49 AD: The Jerusalem Apostolic Council settles Gentile obligations under the Law.\n• 62 AD: Martyrdom of James the Just under High Priest Ananus II.\n• 66–70 AD: Great Jewish Revolt; Titus besieges the city and completely demolishes the Temple.",
    epistles: ["Hebrews", "James"]
